course: biology
lesson: 370
title: Minibeasts in the house: house spiders, silverfish, woodlice, moths and cockroaches
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your home is a small wild world: spiders hunt in the corners, silverfish nibble old books, woodlice hide under flowerpots, moth caterpillars munch wool and dust mites live in your bed.
explain: Most of them want the same things as you: a warm, sheltered place with food and water, and they come out when the lights are off.
explain: Find a big spider in the bath and it looks as if it crawled up the plughole, but it almost certainly fell in while hunting and cannot climb the slippery sides; a towel ramp lets it out.
explain: Many scary stories about house minibeasts are myths, and the best way to keep pests away is not sprays but cleaning crumbs, fixing drips and airing clothes.
fact: A spider in the bath almost certainly fell in while hunting and cannot climb the slippery sides, and a plughole's water trap stops spiders coming up the drain; most spiders have eight legs and eight eyes.
fact: The big spiders seen in houses in autumn are mostly males already living there, walking about looking for partners; giant house spiders can have a leg span of about 7 centimetres.
fact: House spiders eat flies, moths and even other spiders, so they are free pest control.
fact: There is no evidence that people swallow eight spiders a year in their sleep, and a breathing, rumbling, warm mouth is a danger signal that spiders avoid.
fact: Silverfish are wingless insects with three long tails that have looked much the same for an enormously long time; they eat starch (the stuff in flour and pasta), glue, paper and crumbs, like damp dark places and can live a year without food.
fact: Woodlice are not insects but crustaceans, related to crabs and shrimps, with 14 legs in seven pairs, and the pill woodlouse rolls into a ball.
fact: Woodlice breathe with gill-like parts that must stay damp, so they hide in wet dark places, and they can take up water through tiny tubes at the back as well as through the mouth.
fact: Woodlice eat their own poo to recycle copper their bodies need and clean up the garden by eating dead leaves and wood.
fact: Holes in wool clothes are made by clothes moth caterpillars, not the adult moths, which do not eat at all; the caterpillars eat wool, fur and feathers, and washing, airing and freezing woollens gets rid of them.
fact: Scientists filmed in 2024 that moths turn their backs to the brightest light to tell which way is up, so near a lamp they circle and get stuck; warm-coloured street lights trap fewer moths.
fact: Cockroaches breathe through little holes along their body, hide in warm cracks near food and water, and one is reported to live about a week without its head, until it dies of thirst.
fact: Cockroaches can survive much more radiation, the invisible harmful rays from nuclear bombs and accidents, than people, but some other insects take even more, and only about 30 of more than 4,000 kinds of cockroach live in houses.
fact: Dust mites are about a quarter of a millimetre long, eat flakes of dead skin and have eight legs because they are relatives of spiders; their droppings can make some people sneeze and wheeze.
exercise: How did the big spider most likely get into the bath? | It fell in while hunting and cannot climb the slippery sides | It crawled up the plughole from the drains under the house at night | It was born in the bath | It came down the tap
exercise: How can you help a spider out of the bath? | Put in a towel ramp or use a cup and a postcard | Pour hot water on it to wash it down the plughole | Wait until it learns to climb glass | Blow on it very hard
exercise: Why do people see more big spiders indoors in autumn? | Males already living in the house walk about looking for partners | Spiders come in from the snow in autumn | All spiders hatch in September | Spiders follow people home from school
exercise: What do house spiders eat? | Flies, moths and even other spiders | Bread crumbs dropped on the kitchen floor | Dust only | Wool jumpers and old socks
exercise: Is it true that people swallow eight spiders a year in their sleep? | No, there is no evidence and spiders avoid a breathing mouth | Yes, exactly eight every year, scientists have counted them all | Yes, but only children | Yes, and more in winter
exercise: What do silverfish eat? | Starch, glue, paper and crumbs | Only living insects they hunt | Blood from sleeping people and pets | Metal
exercise: How many tails does a silverfish have at the end of its body? | Three | One | None | Eight
exercise: What kind of animal is a woodlouse? | A crustacean, related to crabs and shrimps | An insect, a close relative of beetles and ants | A spider | A worm with tiny legs
exercise: How many legs does a woodlouse have? | 14 | 6 | 8 | 100
exercise: Why must woodlice stay in damp places? | Their gill-like breathing parts must stay damp | They are afraid of light only, not of dryness | They are made almost completely of water inside | They need mud to eat
exercise: Why does a woodlouse eat its own poo? | To recycle copper its body needs | Because it is too lazy to find food | To make itself smell bad to hunters | It never does this, that is just a myth
exercise: What makes holes in wool jumpers? | Clothes moth caterpillars | Adult moths with sharp teeth | Silverfish living in the drawer | Dust mites hiding in the fluff
exercise: How can you get rid of clothes moth eggs and caterpillars? | Washing, airing and freezing woollens | Leaving the jumper in a dark damp drawer | Spilling food on the wool to distract them | Adding more wool jumpers to the same drawer
exercise: Why do moths get stuck circling a lamp, scientists filmed in 2024? | They turn their backs to the brightest light to tell which way is up | They think the lamp is a flower | They want to keep warm because their wings get cold at night | They are trying to eat the light
exercise: Which street lights trap fewer moths? | Warm-coloured lights | Very bright blue-white lights | Flashing orange lights | All lights trap the same number
exercise: How does a cockroach breathe? | Through little holes along its body | Through its mouth only, like a person | Through gills | Through its long antennae on the top of its head
exercise: How many kinds of cockroach live in houses? | About 30 of more than 4,000 kinds | All 4,000 kinds | Only one kind, the big black one | None, they only live in the wild
exercise: What do dust mites eat? | Flakes of dead skin | Wool from our jumpers | Old paper and cardboard | Blood from our fingers
test: «big spider in the bath, it came up the plughole, I'm never showering again» What is true? | It almost certainly fell in; the water trap stops spiders coming up | Spiders swim up drains every night looking for warm baths to sleep in | Spiders live inside the taps | It was sent by the neighbours
test: Count them: a spider in the bath has 8 legs and 8 eyes. How many legs and eyes in all? | 16 | 8 | 14 | 24
test: «you swallow 8 spiders a year in your sleep, it's a fact, it's got a number» Why do made-up facts with numbers sound so believable? | A number makes a story sound measured and scientific, even when nobody counted | Numbers are always checked by scientists before anybody is allowed to say them | Every fact with a number is true | Spiders really count themselves
test: Count them: a boy turns over a flowerpot and finds 3 woodlice. How many legs is that? | 42 | 18 | 24 | 21
test: In a cartoon, a cockroach dances with six legs and sings into a microphone with its mouth. How does a real cockroach breathe? | Through little holes along its body | Through its mouth like a singer | Through its six legs | It does not need air
test: «only cockroaches would survive a nuclear war, everyone knows» What is right? | They take more radiation than people, but other insects take more and many would die | True, cockroaches cannot be hurt by anything, not even fire, poison or being stepped on | True, they are made of metal | False, cockroaches die from any light
test: Why would cleaning crumbs and fixing drips help more against cockroaches than spraying? | Cockroaches need food and water, so without them they leave or die | Cockroaches are scared of clean floors | Spray makes them stronger and bigger, so it never works at all | Drips are where cockroaches lay eggs only
test: «moths ate my mum's wool jumper, they've got tiny teeth» What is true? | The caterpillars did it; adult clothes moths do not eat at all | Adult moths chew holes with sharp teeth hidden under their furry heads | Moths eat only cotton | The jumper wore out by itself
test: Why do clothes moth caterpillars ignore cotton and plastic clothes? | They eat animal fibres like wool, fur and feathers | Cotton is too soft for them | Plastic is poisonous only to moths | They are afraid of bright colours
test: Why do woodlice die quickly on a sunny, dry pavement? | Their breathing parts dry out | The sun burns their wings | They are too cold | They get lost and starve
test: Why does washing bedding hot help people with dust mite allergy? | Hot washing kills mites and removes their droppings | Mites like the smell of soap and leave the bed to find it | Hot water makes mites grow | It makes skin flakes bigger
test: Count them: under a microscope a girl sees a dust mite and a silverfish. How many legs together, if a silverfish is an insect? | 14 | 16 | 12 | 10
exam: Identify the most probable manner in which a large spider came to be present in a bath. | It fell in whilst hunting and is unable to climb the smooth sides | It ascended through the drain from the sewers beneath the dwelling | It was generated spontaneously within the bath | It descended from the tap
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the woodlouse? | It is a crustacean, related to crabs, possessing fourteen legs | It is an insect possessing six legs | It is a spider possessing eight legs | It is a worm devoid of legs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the damage sustained by woollen garments. | It is caused by the larvae of the clothes moth, the adults taking no food | It is caused by the adult moths by means of teeth concealed beneath the head | It is caused by silverfish exclusively | It is caused by the wearing of cotton
exam: Ascertain the validity of the assertion that persons swallow eight spiders annually during sleep. | It is without foundation in evidence | It has been established by careful measurement | It is true only in respect of children | It is true only during the winter months
